* Grammar-Based Fuzzing Scenarios <<root>>

** Scenario A: Basic Regeneration\n(Primitive Types) <<scenario>>
***_ <b>Target:</b> Existing test cases, parameter mutations excluded
***_ <b>Action:</b> Regenerate argument values
***_ <b>Scope:</b> Parameter Generation using Primitives only
**** Number
**** String
**** Boolean
**** BigInt
**** Array

** Scenario B: Advanced Regeneration\n(Complex Types) <<scenario>>
***_ <b>Target:</b> Existing test cases, parameter mutations excluded
***_ <b>Action:</b> Regenerate argument values using expanded grammar
***_ <b>Scope:</b> Parameter Generation using Composite & Reference Types
**** Class Instances / Objects
*****_ Note: Need to research the difference of these two<<note>>
**** Union Types (Type A | Type B)
**** Intersection Types (Type A & Type B)
**** Function / Callback signatures

** Scenario C: Syntactic & Type Boundary Exploration <<scenario>>
***_ <b>Target:</b> Tests focusing on parameter shape and type compliance
***_ <b>Action:</b> Alter parameter metadata and exhaust type constraints
***_ <b>Scope:</b> Type System Boundaries
**** Union & Intersection Exhaustion
*****_ Note: Generating edge-case parameter shapes to fully cover overlapping type definitions (e.g., (A | B) & C)<<note>>
**** Array vs. Tuple Ambiguity
*****_ Note: Fuzzing length and element order to test strict tuple boundaries against open-ended arrays (e.g., [T, U] vs (T | U)[])<<note>>
**** Metadata & Modifier Permutations
*****_ Note: Fuzzing complex combinations of optional (?), defaults, and rest (...) parameters<<note>>
**** Structural Subtyping Injection
*****_ Note: Passing excess properties to test TypeScript's strict object literal resolution rules<<note>>

